    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss",
     ["Should I skip meals to lose weight?", "Is skipping breakfast good for weight loss?"],
     "Skipping meals isn't required and often backfires by making you very hungry later. What matters is your total intake over the day and week. Some people do well with fewer, larger meals and others with regular smaller ones - choose a pattern that keeps you satisfied and lets you include protein and vegetables. Never skip meals to 'make up' for eating."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss",
     ["Can I lose weight without exercise?", "Is diet alone enough to lose weight?"],
     "Yes, weight loss is possible through diet changes alone, because the calorie deficit drives it. However, exercise - especially strength training - helps preserve muscle, improves health and mood, and makes it easier to keep the weight off. Even daily walking adds meaningful benefits."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss",
     ["Why have I stopped losing weight?", "My weight loss has plateaued, what should I do?", "I'm stuck at the same weight for weeks"],
     "Plateaus are normal. As you lose weight your energy needs drop a little, and portions or snacks can creep up unnoticed. Try tracking intake honestly for 1-2 weeks, keep protein high, add more daily steps, keep strength training, and check your sleep and stress. Remember that water shifts can hide fat loss on the scale - also track waist measurements and how clothes fit."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss",
     ["How many calories should I eat to lose weight?", "What calorie intake do I need for weight loss?"],
     "Calorie needs vary a lot with age, size, sex and activity, so there's no single number. A practical approach is to estimate your maintenance intake (for example with a reputable online calculator or by tracking your current intake while your weight is stable) and eat about 300-500 calories less per day, then adjust based on progress. Avoid very low intakes; if you have a medical condition, ask a doctor or registered dietitian for a personalised target."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss",
     ["Does walking help with weight loss?", "Is walking enough to lose weight?"],
     "Yes - walking is one of the most sustainable ways to increase your daily calorie burn. Brisk walking counts as moderate activity, and adding steps throughout the day adds up. Combined with sensible eating and some strength training, regular walking can support steady weight loss."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss",
     ["What foods should I limit to lose weight?", "Which foods should I avoid during weight loss?"],
     "No food is completely forbidden, but some are worth limiting because they are calorie-dense and easy to overeat: sugary drinks (soda, energy drinks, sweetened coffee drinks), sweets and pastries, deep-fried foods, chips and other highly processed snacks, and alcohol. Build most meals around vegetables, fruit, lean protein, legumes and whole grains, and enjoy treats in planned, smaller portions."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss",
     ["Do fat burner pills work?", "Should I take fat burners to lose weight?"],
     "Fat burner supplements generally have weak evidence and some contain high doses of stimulants or undeclared ingredients that can raise heart rate and blood pressure. They are not needed for weight loss. Focus on diet, activity and sleep, and talk to a doctor or pharmacist before taking any weight-loss product, especially if you have a health condition or take medication."),

    # ===================== 3. Muscle building =====================
