    # ===================== 6. Beverages & hydration =====================
    ("hydration", "hydration",
     ["How much water should I drink a day?", "How much water do I need daily?", "What's the daily water requirement?"],
     "The National Academies suggest a total water intake (from all drinks and food) of about 3.7 litres a day for men and 2.7 litres for women. Needs go up with heat, sweating and exercise. A simple check is your urine colour - pale yellow usually means you're well hydrated. Drink regularly through the day and a bit more around workouts."),
    ("hydration", "hydration",
     ["What drinks should I avoid?", "Which drinks are bad for fitness?", "What drinks should I limit?"],
     "Drinks worth limiting are those with lots of added sugar or empty calories: regular soda, energy drinks, sweetened iced teas, fruit punch, sugary coffee drinks and large amounts of juice. Alcohol also adds calories and can disrupt sleep and recovery. Better everyday choices are water, sparkling water, unsweetened tea or coffee, and milk or fortified soy milk."),
    ("hydration", "hydration",
     ["Are sports drinks necessary?", "Should I drink Gatorade during workouts?", "Do I need electrolyte drinks?"],
     "For most workouts under an hour, water is enough. Sports drinks with electrolytes and carbohydrates can help during long sessions (over about an hour), in hot conditions or with heavy sweating. Otherwise they mainly add sugar and calories."),
    ("hydration", "hydration",
     ["Is coffee good before a workout?", "Can I drink coffee before exercise?"],
     "Yes, for most healthy adults a cup of coffee about 30-60 minutes before exercise can modestly improve performance and alertness. The FDA notes up to about 400 mg of caffeine a day is generally not associated with negative effects for healthy adults. Avoid it late in the day if it affects your sleep, and skip it if you're sensitive to caffeine or have a heart condition."),
    ("hydration", "hydration",
     ["Are energy drinks bad?", "Is it okay to drink energy drinks before the gym?"],
     "Energy drinks are often high in sugar and caffeine, and drinking several can push caffeine intake too high, causing a racing heart, anxiety or poor sleep. Occasional use by healthy adults is usually fine, but they're not needed for good workouts. Water and a small snack, or a cup of coffee, are simpler options."),
    ("hydration", "hydration",
     ["How do I know if I'm dehydrated?", "What are the signs of dehydration?"],
     "Common signs of mild dehydration are thirst, dark yellow urine, a dry mouth, headache, fatigue and dizziness. Drink fluids and rest somewhere cool. Severe symptoms - confusion, fainting, a very rapid heartbeat, or no urine for many hours - need urgent medical attention."),
    ("hydration", "hydration",
     ["Does alcohol affect my fitness?", "Can I drink beer and still build muscle?"],
     "Alcohol adds calories, can disrupt sleep and may slow recovery and muscle repair, especially in larger amounts. If you drink, keep it moderate (guidelines suggest no more than 2 drinks a day for men and 1 for women, and less is better), avoid drinking right after hard training, and stay hydrated."),
    ("hydration", "hydration",
     ["Is coconut water good for hydration?", "Coconut water vs water?"],
     "Coconut water contains water plus some potassium and a little natural sugar, so it can be a pleasant way to hydrate. For most people and most workouts, plain water does the job just as well and has no calories."),
    ("hydration", "hydration",
     ["Can you drink too much water?", "Is drinking a lot of water dangerous?"],
     "Yes - drinking very large amounts of plain water in a short time, especially during long endurance events, can dilute blood sodium (hyponatremia), which can be dangerous. For everyday life, drink to thirst and aim for pale yellow urine. During very long events, include electrolytes and avoid forcing fluids."),

    # ===================== 7. Pre/Post workout =====================
    ("pre_post_workout", "nutrition_question",
     ["What should I eat before a workout?", "Best pre-workout meal?", "What to eat before going to the gym?"],
     "Eat a meal with carbohydrates and some protein about 1-3 hours before training - for example oats with milk and banana, rice with chicken and vegetables, or whole-wheat toast with eggs. If you only have 30-60 minutes, choose a small, easily digested snack like a banana, yogurt or a rice cake. Avoid very heavy, fatty or high-fiber meals right before training."),
    ("pre_post_workout", "nutrition_question",
     ["What should I eat after a workout?", "Best post-workout meal?", "What should I eat after working out?"],
     "After training, aim for a meal or snack with about 20-40 g of protein plus some carbohydrates to support muscle repair and refuel. Good options: chicken with rice and vegetables, Greek yogurt with fruit, eggs on whole-wheat toast, a tofu or lentil dish with rice, tuna on whole-grain bread, or a milk smoothie with banana. Eating within a few hours is fine for most people."),
    ("pre_post_workout", "nutrition_question",
     ["Is it okay to work out on an empty stomach?", "Can I exercise without eating?", "Fasted cardio, good or bad?"],
     "Training on an empty stomach is fine for many people, especially for light or moderate morning workouts. Some people feel weak or dizzy without food, and performance in long or intense sessions is usually better when fueled. If you train fasted, have a balanced meal afterwards. People with diabetes or other conditions should follow their doctor's advice."),
    ("pre_post_workout", "nutrition_question",
     ["Do I need a protein shake after the gym?", "Is a protein shake necessary after a workout?"],
     "No - a protein shake is just a convenient option. A regular meal or snack with protein, like yogurt, eggs, chicken, fish, tofu or milk, works just as well. Shakes are useful if you're on the go or struggling to reach your daily protein target."),
    ("pre_post_workout", "nutrition_question",
     ["How long should I wait to exercise after eating?", "Can I work out right after a meal?"],
     "After a full meal, wait about 2-3 hours before intense exercise. After a small snack, 30-60 minutes is usually enough. Light walking right after eating is fine and can even help digestion."),
    ("pre_post_workout", "nutrition_question",
     ["Is chocolate milk good after a workout?", "Chocolate milk as recovery drink?"],
     "Chocolate milk can be a convenient recovery drink because it combines protein and carbohydrates with fluid. It also contains added sugar, so it makes the most sense after longer or harder sessions. Plain milk with a banana is a similar option with less added sugar."),
    ("pre_post_workout", "nutrition_question",
     ["What should I eat before a morning run?", "Breakfast before running?"],
     "If you run early, a small, easy-to-digest snack 30-60 minutes before - such as a banana, a slice of toast with honey, or a few dates - plus some water works well. For short easy runs some people prefer running fasted. Have a proper breakfast with protein and carbs afterwards."),

